# tracker-audit-12-checks
A free twelve-check audit sheet for a hand-kept client or matter tracker, plus the published article
and the script that built the deliberately broken test file. Written for a small business owner in a
title agency, an insurance agency, a small law firm or a financial advisory practice.
Python, pandas, openpyxl, matplotlib and the standard library only. The spreadsheet uses only
functions that ship with Excel 2019 and Google Sheets. No paid services, no paid APIs, no extra
dependencies without asking.
## Rules for every change in this repo
1. Every number traces to a data file.
No figure appears in prose, in a caption, in alt text or in the video unless it is printed by
build_tracker.py into summary_output.txt. If a number is not in that file, write [FIGURE NEEDED]
instead of a value. Never estimate, round up or illustrate with a plausible number.
2. Say when data is illustrative.
The tracker in this repo is synthetic and is not a client file. Every one of its 40 defects was
injected deliberately by script with a fixed seed. Keep the illustrative label in the article,
the README, injection_spec.md, both chart captions and on screen in the video. The 40 count is a
property of this file. Never write it, or any per-class count, as a rate found in real
businesses.
3. Stay in time, not money.
The gap between the reported total and the actual total is stated in this file's own units and
stops there. There is no revenue, commission, loss, cost-of-error or value-at-risk field anywhere
in this project, not even unused, and no chart axis or caption converts the gap into what an
error costs a business. If a suggestion reaches for cost or revenue, drop it.
4. Write about the reader, not about us.
The subject is the reader's own tracker. No client names, no pricing, no testimonials, no Beagle
metrics, no throughput or headcount figures, no capacity or delivery promises. The audit sheet
recommends no purchase anywhere.
5. Your name, your call.
Assets carry builder names. G M Shashank Showrya and Shreya Badakundri must not be quoted by
anyone else; they write and narrate their own work only. Do not alter credits, bylines or an end
card, do not put words in anyone's mouth, and do not mark an asset approved.
## Spreadsheet conventions
- Excel 2019 compatibility is a hard constraint. Do not use XLOOKUP, UNIQUE, FILTER, SORT, LET,
LAMBDA, TEXTSPLIT, TRIMRANGE or any spilled dynamic array. They return #NAME? in Excel 2019.
- Allowed: SUMPRODUCT, COUNTIF, COUNTIFS, ISTEXT, ISNUMBER, SEARCH, TRIM, CLEAN, SUBSTITUTE, EXACT,
UPPER, PROPER, SUBTOTAL, AGGREGATE, TEXTJOIN, COUNTA, MIN, MAX, IFERROR.
- Every check formula must return the same result in Google Sheets. Sheets-only conveniences
(QUERY, REGEXMATCH, ARRAYFORMULA) may appear as an optional alternative column, never as the
primary instruction.
- Fixed ranges to row 5000 with a COUNTA guard, so a reader with 300 rows gets no false flags.
- If a check is cut, retitle the sheet, the article and the video to the number that actually works.
## Script conventions
- Fixed seed, random.seed(20261120), committed. Output must be reproducible on another machine.
- Every injection count lives in a module-level CONFIG dict with a comment saying why.
No magic numbers inline.
- The clean control copy and the broken copy are generated from the same base rows in one run.
- gap_by_class.csv is computed by re-injecting one class at a time onto a fresh clean copy, never by
subtracting classes from the fully broken file. The unattributable remainder is a row labelled
interaction.
- One chart script produces one PNG: chart_a_counts.py to chart_a.png, chart_b_gap.py to
chart_b.png. Both PNGs at 2x. Captions sit below the axes, not as a title inside the image.
- No hard-coded absolute paths. Paths are relative to the repo root.
- When you add or assume a dependency, a Python version or a directory, add it to README.md in the
same change.